yle-module-scss-module__nbagBW__articleWrap" id=3D"content"><article class=
=3D"style-module-scss-module__nbagBW__learnContent"><header><h1>Longest Sub=
string With Unique Characters</h1></header><div class=3D"style-module-scss-=
module__ubvNPq__problemDescription"><p>Given a string, determine the length=
 of its longest substring that consists only of <strong>unique characters</=
strong>.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><pre><code class=3D"hljs langua=
ge-python">Input: s =3D <span class=3D"hljs-string">'abcba'</span>
Output: <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>
</code></pre><p>Explanation: Substring "abc" is the longest substring of le=
ngth 3 that contains unique characters ("cba" also fits this description).<=
/p></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>The brute force approach involves examining all possible substrings and =
checking if any consist of exclusively unique characters. Let=E2=80=99s bre=
ak down this approach:</p>
<ul>
<li>Checking a substring for uniqueness can be done in <span class=3D"katex=
"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/M=
athML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><m=
o stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex=
">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" ari=
a-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"heigh=
t:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=
=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span cla=
ss=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span=
></span> time by scanning the substring and using a <strong>hash set</stron=
g> to keep track of each character, where n denotes the length of s. If we =
encounter a character already in the hash set, we know it=E2=80=99s a dupli=
cate character.</li>
<li>Iterating through all possible substrings takes <span class=3D"katex"><=
span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/Math=
ML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi=
><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span c=
lass=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=
=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span cl=
ass=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span clas=
s=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n<=
/span><span class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-=
r"><span class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height:0.8141em"><span style=3D"top:-3.06=
3em;margin-right:0.05em"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em"></sp=
an><span class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtig=
ht">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D=
"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time.</li>
</ul>
<p>This means the brute force approach would take <span class=3D"katex"><sp=
an class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML=
"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><=
mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D=
"application/x-tex">O(n^3)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span clas=
s=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"s=
trut" style=3D"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=
=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=
=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</=
span><span class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r=
"><span class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height:0.8141em"><span style=3D"top:-3.063=
em;margin-right:0.05em"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em"></spa=
n><span class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtigh=
t">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D"=
mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time overall. This is quite slow, lar=
gely because we look through every substring. Is there a way to reduce the =
number of substrings we examine?</p>
<p><strong>Sliding window</strong><br>
<!-- -->Sliding window approaches can be quite useful for problems that inv=
olve substrings. In particular, because we=E2=80=99re looking for the longe=
st substring that satisfies a specific condition (i.e., contains unique cha=
racters), a <strong>dynamic sliding window</strong> algorithm might be the =
way to go, as discussed in the introduction.</p>
<p>We can categorize any window in two ways. A window either:</p>
<ol>
<li>Consists only of unique characters (a window with no duplicate characte=
rs).</li>
</ol>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a visual depiction of a data structure or algor=
ithm concept related to character uniqueness.  A light-blue rectangular box=
 contains the characters 'a', 'b', and 'c' arranged horizontally.  A gray l=
